1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,763
That question reaches a CTO's desk in an
AI and ML team, usually followed by three

2
00:00:09,803 --> 00:00:10,086
more.

3
00:00:10,426 --> 00:00:15,158
"The clone takes twenty minutes and the
repository holds four hundred lines of

4
00:00:15,198 --> 00:00:20,236
Python." "The training job in CI crashed
while loading the weights: the file is 132

5
00:00:20,276 --> 00:00:25,375
bytes and starts with the word version."
"Our storage bill for this repository keeps

6
00:00:25,415 --> 00:00:28,372
growing, and nobody has
added a model in months."

7
00:00:28,712 --> 00:00:33,653
The first two are Git doing what it was
designed to do, with content it wasn't

8
00:00:33,693 --> 00:00:38,762
designed for. The third is Git LFS working
as designed on a machine that doesn't

9
00:00:38,802 --> 00:00:42,018
have it. The fourth is how
GitHub accounts for LFS.

10
00:00:42,058 --> 00:00:46,616
To answer any of them you need to know
one thing: what LFS stores where.

11
00:00:46,656 --> 00:00:50,830
Hold on to the first question.
You'll answer it in the first demo.

12
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:56,462
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:56,502 --> 00:01:01,551
Git LFS, Large File Storage, isn't part
of Git. It's a separate program, git-lfs,

14
00:01:01,591 --> 00:01:06,514
that plugs into Git through two mechanisms
you already know: a clean and smudge

15
00:01:06,554 --> 00:01:11,540
filter selected by .gitattributes, the
file that attaches settings to paths, and

16
00:01:11,580 --> 00:01:16,755
hooks, the programs Git runs at fixed points.
So this video doesn't teach you a new

17
00:01:16,795 --> 00:01:20,335
mechanism. It shows you what
this particular filter does.

18
00:01:20,675 --> 00:01:26,285
The project is transcriber, a speech-to-text
service: a little code and one acoustic

19
00:01:26,325 --> 00:01:31,732
model file. The lab uses stand-in files
of 1.2 to 1.3 megabytes so that the demos

20
00:01:31,772 --> 00:01:36,910
run in a second. Real models are a
thousand times larger and behave the same.

21
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:40,803
One statement about evidence,
as the textbook makes it.

22
00:01:40,843 --> 00:01:46,158
The remote in this chapter is a bare
repository reached through a file path, which

23
00:01:46,198 --> 00:01:49,816
git-lfs 3.7.1 supports with
a built-in transfer adapter.

24
00:01:49,856 --> 00:01:55,303
So tracking, pushing, cloning, fetching,
pruning and migrating are real transcripts.

25
00:01:55,343 --> 00:02:00,593
Anything that needs an LFS server could
not be run: the HTTP API, authentication,

26
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:06,015
file locking, and all GitHub limits and
billing. Those parts are described from the

27
00:02:06,055 --> 00:02:07,778
documentation and labelled.

28
00:02:09,618 --> 00:02:15,032
explain what large binary files do to every
clone, and why deleting them later does

29
00:02:15,072 --> 00:02:15,623
not help;

30
00:02:15,963 --> 00:02:21,536
say what Git stores for an LFS-tracked path
and what LFS stores, and where each is;

31
00:02:21,876 --> 00:02:23,154
read a pointer file;

32
00:02:23,494 --> 00:02:28,347
explain which two Git mechanisms LFS uses
and which parts travel with a clone;

33
00:02:28,687 --> 00:02:32,554
state what git lfs track does not
do for files already committed.

34
00:02:34,394 --> 00:02:39,801
Why LFS exists. In one sentence: Git keeps
every version of every file forever and

35
00:02:39,841 --> 00:02:45,314
gives every clone all of them, which is
right for source code and ruinous for large

36
00:02:45,354 --> 00:02:46,709
binaries that change.

37
00:02:47,049 --> 00:02:52,090
Packfiles, the files Git packs its objects
into, store similar objects as deltas,

38
00:02:52,130 --> 00:02:55,478
and that helps when versions
share long runs of bytes.

39
00:02:55,518 --> 00:03:00,622
Retrained weights, compressed archives,
images and audio share almost nothing from

40
00:03:00,662 --> 00:03:05,829
one version to the next. So each version
costs its full size, and zlib can't shrink

41
00:03:05,869 --> 00:03:11,098
data that's already dense. The cost grows
with the number of versions, and it's paid

42
00:03:11,138 --> 00:03:13,482
again by every clone and every CI job.

43
00:03:13,822 --> 00:03:18,675
Deleting the file adds a commit. The old
blobs, the objects that hold the file's

44
00:03:18,715 --> 00:03:21,978
content, are still reachable
from the earlier commits.

45
00:03:22,018 --> 00:03:26,872
A hosting platform that rejects a large
blob rejects it because it's in a commit

46
00:03:26,912 --> 00:03:31,826
being pushed, not because it's in the
latest tree. Only a history rewrite removes

47
00:03:31,866 --> 00:03:32,010
it.

48
00:03:32,350 --> 00:03:37,442
LFS changes one thing: Git stores a small
text file in place of the binary, and the

49
00:03:37,482 --> 00:03:41,956
binary goes to a separate store that's
downloaded per version, on demand.

50
00:03:42,296 --> 00:03:48,385
The pointer and the two filters. In one
sentence: for a path that's tracked by LFS,

51
00:03:48,425 --> 00:03:54,440
the blob in Git is a three-line pointer
containing the SHA-256 and the size of the

52
00:03:54,480 --> 00:03:59,979
real content. SHA-256 is a checksum, a
fingerprint computed from the bytes.

53
00:04:00,019 --> 00:04:05,961
The real content lives in an LFS object
store, locally under .git/lfs/objects and

54
00:04:06,001 --> 00:04:08,545
remotely beside the Git repository.

55
00:04:08,885 --> 00:04:12,543
Now precisely: two filter
commands do the exchange.

56
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:18,560
Clean, run by git add, reads the file
content, computes its SHA-256, and stores the

57
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:24,432
content in .git/lfs/objects, in a path
made of the first two hex digits, the next

58
00:04:24,472 --> 00:04:28,709
two, and the full hash. Then
it hands Git the pointer text.

59
00:04:28,749 --> 00:04:31,899
Git hashes and stores
the pointer as a blob.

60
00:04:32,239 --> 00:04:37,209
Smudge, run by checkout, reads the pointer
from the blob, finds the object in the

61
00:04:37,249 --> 00:04:42,158
local store, downloads it from the remote
if it isn't there, and writes the real

62
00:04:42,198 --> 00:04:44,013
content into the working tree.

63
00:04:44,353 --> 00:04:50,192
The pointer format is fixed by the
specification: UTF-8 text, one key and value per

64
00:04:50,232 --> 00:04:56,001
line, version first and the other keys in
alphabetical order, less than 1024 bytes

65
00:04:56,041 --> 00:05:01,667
in total. The required keys are version,
oid with the hash method as prefix, and

66
00:05:01,707 --> 00:05:02,022
size.

67
00:05:02,362 --> 00:05:05,476
The .gitattributes line.
git lfs track writes it.

68
00:05:05,816 --> 00:05:09,648
Three rules follow from this being
an ordinary attributes file.

69
00:05:09,688 --> 00:05:14,320
Commit .gitattributes: it's the only
part of the setup that travels with the

70
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:19,237
repository. The filter definition is local
configuration and the hooks are local

71
00:05:19,277 --> 00:05:23,293
files, and a clone gets neither.
Patterns follow gitignore syntax.

72
00:05:23,633 --> 00:05:28,420
Quote them in the shell, or the shell
expands the pattern to the files that exist

73
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:33,365
today and git lfs track records those names.
And tracking applies from the next git

74
00:05:33,405 --> 00:05:38,191
add. git lfs track edits a text file. It
doesn't convert a file that's already in

75
00:05:38,231 --> 00:05:39,622
the index or in history.

76
00:05:39,962 --> 00:05:44,389
When not to reach for LFS is a topic
of its own two videos from now.

77
00:05:44,429 --> 00:05:49,513
For today, note the limit that the design
implies: the LFS content isn't a Git

78
00:05:49,553 --> 00:05:54,375
object. git cat-file, git fsck, git gc
and git push know nothing about it.

79
00:05:56,215 --> 00:06:00,946
The textbook's analogy: a coat check. You
hand over the coat and carry a numbered

80
00:06:00,986 --> 00:06:05,775
ticket. The ticket goes wherever you go,
and the coat stays in the cloakroom until

81
00:06:05,815 --> 00:06:09,132
someone presents the ticket.
Git history carries tickets.

82
00:06:09,472 --> 00:06:14,429
The analogy breaks in two places. The number
on the ticket is computed from the coat

83
00:06:14,469 --> 00:06:17,760
itself, so the same content
always gets the same ticket.

84
00:06:17,800 --> 00:06:22,637
And a ticket can be copied to a place that
has no cloakroom attendant, where it is

85
00:06:22,677 --> 00:06:23,946
only a piece of paper.

86
00:06:24,286 --> 00:06:29,185
Keep that second break in mind. A machine
without the LFS client receives the

87
00:06:29,225 --> 00:06:34,381
tickets, because tickets are ordinary Git
blobs, and nobody there exchanges them.

88
00:06:34,421 --> 00:06:37,973
That's the 132-byte model
file, and it's the next video.

89
00:06:38,313 --> 00:06:44,240
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Draw
three boxes: working tree, Git objects,

90
00:06:44,280 --> 00:06:50,062
LFS store. For a model of 1,200,000 bytes,
write in each box what you predict it

91
00:06:50,102 --> 00:06:50,499
holds.

92
00:06:56,839 --> 00:07:01,018
Check your drawing. Left: the
real file, 1,200,000 bytes.

93
00:07:01,058 --> 00:07:07,160
Middle: what Git stores, a blob of 132
bytes. Right: where the real bytes are kept,

94
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:13,081
in a file named by their own SHA-256. The
two captions say who moves each store.

95
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:17,596
Git moves the middle one. The
LFS client moves the right one.

96
00:07:19,436 --> 00:07:24,727
Predict: the working tree has one model.
How many model blobs does the repository

97
00:07:24,767 --> 00:07:26,899
have? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

98
00:07:31,739 --> 00:07:34,462
Three, one per version, each stored whole.

99
00:07:34,802 --> 00:07:38,535
A teammate's clone downloads
all three, to check out one.

100
00:07:38,575 --> 00:07:42,574
Now the reflex from the opening:
delete the model and commit.

101
00:07:42,614 --> 00:07:47,804
A quick quiz. How many of the three big
blobs are left afterwards: none, or all

102
00:07:47,844 --> 00:07:49,326
three? Say it out loud.

103
00:07:54,166 --> 00:07:59,815
All three. The deleting commit says "0
insertions, 0 deletions" and "delete mode".

104
00:08:00,155 --> 00:08:05,886
And the three blobs, 1.3, 1.25 and 1.2
megabytes, are still there, reachable from

105
00:08:05,926 --> 00:08:11,658
the earlier commits. That's the answer to
question 1, the one you were holding on

106
00:08:11,698 --> 00:08:11,872
to.

107
00:08:14,712 --> 00:08:20,277
A clean command, a smudge command, a process
variant that handles all files of one

108
00:08:20,317 --> 00:08:24,241
command in a single process,
and filter.lfs.required=true.

109
00:08:24,581 --> 00:08:29,234
You know that last key from the filters
video: if the filter fails, the Git command

110
00:08:29,274 --> 00:08:33,645
fails. You also know its limit: it's part
of a definition that a clone doesn't

111
00:08:33,685 --> 00:08:34,098
receive.

112
00:08:34,438 --> 00:08:39,827
The filter definition says how to convert.
.gitattributes says which paths get

113
00:08:39,867 --> 00:08:43,308
converted. 🟢 SAFE: git lfs
track writes that line.

114
00:08:43,648 --> 00:08:48,076
One line in .gitattributes, with the
four attributes from the table, and git

115
00:08:48,116 --> 00:08:52,897
check-attr confirms them for the model
path. Notice the quotes around the pattern.

116
00:08:53,237 --> 00:08:57,574
Now add a model of 1,200,000 bytes and
commit it together with .gitattributes.

117
00:08:57,914 --> 00:09:04,994
Remember the first characters
of the checksum: 0269885262.

118
00:09:05,334 --> 00:09:10,669
Predict two numbers. The size of the blob
that Git stored for models/acoustic.onnx.

119
00:09:10,709 --> 00:09:14,296
And the size of the file in
the working tree. I'll wait.

120
00:09:19,136 --> 00:09:25,799
The blob is 132 bytes of text. Read the
three lines: version and a URL, then oid

121
00:09:25,839 --> 00:09:31,162
sha256: and the checksum that
shasum printed, then size 1200000.

122
00:09:31,502 --> 00:09:36,715
The working tree still has the real file.
The clean filter changes what goes into

123
00:09:36,755 --> 00:09:42,162
the repository, not what is on your disk.
If you predicted 132 bytes in both places,

124
00:09:42,202 --> 00:09:43,654
that's a natural guess.

125
00:09:43,994 --> 00:09:50,082
The content is under .git/lfs/objects/02/69/,
in a file named by its own SHA-256.

126
00:09:50,122 --> 00:09:56,286
It isn't a Git object. git lfs ls-files
lists the LFS-tracked paths of the current

127
00:09:56,326 --> 00:10:02,641
commit. The asterisk means the working tree
file holds the real content, and a minus

128
00:10:02,681 --> 00:10:08,921
sign would mean it holds a pointer. git lfs
pointer --file computes the pointer for

129
00:10:08,961 --> 00:10:11,342
a file without storing anything.

130
00:10:11,682 --> 00:10:13,978
A new version of the
model, not yet staged.

131
00:10:17,318 --> 00:10:22,732
git diff shows the difference between two
pointers: an oid line and a size line.

132
00:10:22,772 --> 00:10:28,322
No diff driver named lfs is defined, so
this is all a diff of an LFS file can say.

133
00:10:28,362 --> 00:10:31,390
git lfs status adds what
a push would upload.

134
00:10:31,730 --> 00:10:38,707
New bytes, so a new ticket:
0269885 has become f8719e1.

135
00:10:39,047 --> 00:10:40,699
And the second mechanism: hooks.

136
00:10:41,039 --> 00:10:45,984
Each hook has two lines. The first checks
that git-lfs is on the path and prints an

137
00:10:46,024 --> 00:10:50,667
explanation if it isn't. That's the rule
from the hooks video about hooks that

138
00:10:50,707 --> 00:10:55,712
depend on tools. The second hands over to
git lfs. The pre-push hook is what uploads

139
00:10:55,752 --> 00:10:57,273
the content when you push.

140
00:11:04,113 --> 00:11:05,761
Five mistakes to watch for.

141
00:11:06,101 --> 00:11:09,165
Deleting a large file to get
a rejected push through.

142
00:11:09,205 --> 00:11:14,025
Root cause: the blob is still reachable
from the earlier commits being pushed; only

143
00:11:14,065 --> 00:11:15,724
a history rewrite removes it.

144
00:11:16,064 --> 00:11:20,032
Running git lfs track and assuming
existing files are converted.

145
00:11:20,072 --> 00:11:25,043
Root cause: it edits .gitattributes;
conversion happens at the next git add, and

146
00:11:25,083 --> 00:11:28,863
nothing changes for files already
in the index or in history.

147
00:11:29,203 --> 00:11:34,035
An unquoted pattern in git lfs track. Root
cause: the shell expands it to the files

148
00:11:34,075 --> 00:11:38,144
that exist today, and those names
are recorded instead of the pattern.

149
00:11:38,484 --> 00:11:43,719
Not committing .gitattributes. Root cause:
it is the only part of the LFS setup that

150
00:11:43,759 --> 00:11:45,477
travels with the repository.

151
00:11:45,817 --> 00:11:51,599
Expecting git fsck, git gc or git
push to look after the model content.

152
00:11:51,639 --> 00:11:58,406
Root cause: LFS objects are files under
.git/lfs/objects, not Git objects; only the

153
00:11:58,446 --> 00:11:59,308
pointer is.

154
00:12:01,148 --> 00:12:06,321
Now, out of the lab. A repository holds
an evaluation harness and three reference

155
00:12:06,361 --> 00:12:10,441
checkpoints of a few hundred
megabytes that change twice a year.

156
00:12:10,481 --> 00:12:15,654
Before LFS, every retraining added a full
copy of each checkpoint to every clone,

157
00:12:15,694 --> 00:12:19,709
and the CI jobs that only lint
the code downloaded all of them.

158
00:12:20,049 --> 00:12:24,976
With LFS, a clone downloads the code and
the pointers at once, and each checkpoint

159
00:12:25,016 --> 00:12:29,942
only when a checkout needs it. A CI job
that doesn't need the checkpoints can skip

160
00:12:29,982 --> 00:12:34,848
them. The team's review checklist gained
one line: a pull request that adds a new

161
00:12:34,888 --> 00:12:39,936
binary type must also add its pattern to
.gitattributes, in the same commit, and the

162
00:12:39,976 --> 00:12:43,146
checksum in the pointer is
what the model card cites.

163
00:12:44,986 --> 00:12:50,131
Your turn. Do Lab 15.3,
"Inspect an LFS pointer", in

164
00:12:50,171 --> 00:12:54,319
lab-manual/m15-submodules-subtrees-lfs.md.

165
00:12:54,659 --> 00:13:00,049
Before you commit the tracked file, predict
the three lines of the pointer: compute

166
00:13:00,089 --> 00:13:05,544
the checksum yourself and write down the
size. Predict the size that git cat-file -s

167
00:13:05,584 --> 00:13:10,320
will print for the path, and the path
of the file under .git/lfs/objects.

168
00:13:10,660 --> 00:13:17,457
The challenge is Exercise 15.6, Level 2,
command prediction, "Tracked too late", in

169
00:13:17,497 --> 00:13:21,081
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

170
00:13:24,921 --> 00:13:29,380
Answer out loud. A strong answer names
two stores and never blurs them.

171
00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:34,640
For Git's side it says what kind of object
the path is, what its content looks like

172
00:13:34,680 --> 00:13:39,836
and roughly how large it is. For the LFS
side it says how the content is addressed

173
00:13:39,876 --> 00:13:41,673
and gives the local location.

174
00:13:42,013 --> 00:13:46,552
It says which program moves each store and
at which moment, and it's careful about

175
00:13:46,592 --> 00:13:50,852
the remote: what is known from a run
against a local remote and what a hosted

176
00:13:50,892 --> 00:13:55,375
service does according to its documentation.
It may add which part of the setup a

177
00:13:55,415 --> 00:13:57,720
fresh clone receives and which it doesn't.

178
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:05,458
Let's land this. One path, two sizes: the
real bytes in your working tree, and a

179
00:14:05,498 --> 00:14:07,239
132-byte pointer in Git.

180
00:14:07,579 --> 00:14:26,295
You should now be able to say:

181
00:14:26,635 --> 00:14:31,781
Git gives every clone every version of
every file, and binaries that change do not

182
00:14:31,821 --> 00:14:36,081
delta or compress, so each version
costs its full size for everyone.

183
00:14:36,421 --> 00:14:40,479
Deleting a large file later does
not remove its blobs from history.

184
00:14:40,819 --> 00:14:47,648
For an LFS-tracked path Git stores a pointer
of about 130 bytes with the SHA-256 and

185
00:14:47,688 --> 00:14:53,209
the size; the content is under
.git/lfs/objects, named by that hash.

186
00:14:53,549 --> 00:14:58,730
LFS is a clean and smudge filter selected
by .gitattributes, plus hooks; only

187
00:14:58,770 --> 00:15:01,171
.gitattributes travels with a clone.

188
00:15:01,511 --> 00:15:06,104
git lfs track affects files added
afterwards, not files already committed.

189
00:15:07,944 --> 00:15:16,326
Read sections 22.1 to 22.4 of Chapter 22.
Do Exercise 15.2, Level 1, "A first LFS

190
00:15:16,366 --> 00:15:22,253
pointer", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

191
00:15:22,593 --> 00:15:28,081
Today you read a pointer file and matched
its checksum by hand, and you can now say

192
00:15:28,121 --> 00:15:32,343
what LFS stores where. Practise
with the lab before you move on.

193
00:15:32,383 --> 00:15:36,339
Next time: Git LFS day to day,
and that 132-byte model file.

194
00:15:36,379 --> 00:15:39,802
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

195
